# frozen_string_literal: true

# The reference toolchain's gem closure, pinned EXACTLY.
#
# These gems decide the bytes of every committed reference PDF, so a version that is free to drift is
# a reference that is free to drift. `prawn`, `pdf-core` and `ttfunk` lay out the PDF objects and
# subset the fonts; `prawn-svg` rasterises figures; `rouge` colours source blocks. A machine that
# resolved any of them differently would regenerate references that no longer match the corpus, and
# the parity suite would report a fidelity regression that is really just a different gem.
#
# Every version here is the one the closure ALREADY resolved to when this was pinned, verified by
# regenerating a fixture and comparing SHA-256 against the committed reference — so pinning changed
# nothing. Transitive versions are frozen by the committed `Gemfile.lock` beside this file; the image
# installs with `--frozen`, so a lock that no longer matches this file fails the build instead of
# silently resolving something new.
#
# Bump deliberately, and regenerate the whole corpus in the same commit.
source "https://rubygems.org"

# The engine. MUST stay in lockstep with the version the wasm build bundles (packages/asciidoc-pdf),
# because parity compares the two toolchains against each other.
gem "asciidoctor-pdf", "2.3.24"

# SVG figures. Note this is NOT the newest release: it is the version asciidoctor-pdf 2.3.24 resolves
# to, and the one the wasm engine loads. Installing it unpinned put TWO copies in the image.
gem "prawn-svg", "0.34.2"

# Source-block highlighting. MUST stay in lockstep with the wasm engine's rouge
# (packages/asciidoc-pdf/ruby/Gemfile), for the same reason asciidoctor-pdf must: parity compares the
# two toolchains, so a rouge only ONE side has is measured as a fidelity defect in the app.
#
# It was 5.0.0 here while the engine shipped 4.7.0, and the two disagree about exactly one thing in
# this corpus — rouge 5's Python overhaul (rouge-ruby/rouge#2271) lexes the module in `from X import`
# as `Name::Namespace`, which `asciidoctor_pdf_default` inks #bb0066 bold, where 4.7.0 emits a bare
# `Name` the theme leaves at the code colour. So the `highlighting` and `highlighting-themed`
# references were drawing a word in a colour and weight the shipped app cannot produce, and the Print
# fidelity oracle was scoring the preview against it.
#
# The convergence has to be DOWNWARD, and this is the constraint that decides it: rouge >= 5.0
# declares a runtime dependency on the `strscan` GEM (`~> 3.1`), and Ruby 3.3's built-in strscan is
# 3.0.9, so bundler must vendor strscan 3.1.8 — which ships a C extension (`ext/strscan/extconf.rb`).
# The wasm engine's closure may contain no native extension (WASI has no host extension loading), and
# `packages/asciidoc-pdf/ruby/build-wasm.sh` fails closed on exactly that. Verified by resolving the
# engine's Gemfile against `rouge "~> 5.0"`: strscan 3.1.8 enters the closure and trips the gate.
# So rouge cannot cross 5.0 on the engine side, and this side must not cross it alone.
#
# 4.7.0 is the newest 4.x, and unlike 5.x it has no runtime dependencies at all.
gem "rouge", "4.7.0"

# Citations and diagrams, for the two fixtures whose references cannot be built without them. Kept in
# the same closure rather than a second image so there is ONE reference toolchain: a second image is
# a second set of resolved versions, which is exactly how the corpus would drift apart.
gem "asciidoctor-bibtex", "0.9.0"
gem "asciidoctor-diagram", "3.2.1"
